1
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Some legacies must end.

2
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:02,000
American History X is a drama film directed by Tony Kaye.

3
00:11:54,000 --> 00:12:00,000
Derek Vineyard is paroled after serving 3 years in
prison for killing two African-American men.

4
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:58,000
Through his brother, Danny Vineyard's narration, we learn that before going to prison, Derek was a skinhead and
the leader of a violent white supremacist gang that committed acts of racial crime throughout L.A.

5
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:56,000
and his actions greatly influenced Danny.

6
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:54,000
Reformed and fresh out of prison, Derek severs contact with the gang and becomes
determined to keep Danny from going down the same violent path as he did.

7
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:52,000
Derek, played by Edward Norton.

8
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:50,000
Danny, played by Edward Furlong.

9
00:35:42,000 --> 00:35:48,000
Doris, played by Beverly D'Angelo.

10
00:39:40,000 --> 00:39:46,000
Davina, played by Jennifer Lien.

11
00:43:38,000 --> 00:43:44,000
Seth, played by Ethan Suplee.

12
00:47:36,000 --> 00:47:42,000
Stacey, played by Fairuza Balk.

13
00:51:34,000 --> 00:51:40,000
American History X is a 1998 American crime drama film directed by Tony Kaye
(in his feature directorial debut) and written by David McKenna.

14
00:55:32,000 --> 00:55:38,000
The film stars Edward Norton and Edward Furlong as two brothers from Los Angeles
who are involved in the white power skinhead and neo-Nazi movements.

15
00:59:30,000 --> 00:59:36,000
The older brother (Norton) serves three years in prison for voluntary manslaughter, is rehabilitated during
this time, and then tries to prevent his brother (Furlong) from being indoctrinated further.

16
01:03:28,000 --> 01:03:34,000
The supporting cast includes Fairuza Balk, Stacy Keach, Elliott
Gould, Avery Brooks, Ethan Suplee and Beverly D'Angelo.

17
01:07:26,000 --> 01:07:32,000
McKenna wrote the script based on his own childhood and
experiences of growing up in San Diego.

18
01:11:24,000 --> 01:11:30,000
He sold the script to New Line Cinema, which was impressed by the writing.

19
01:15:22,000 --> 01:15:28,000
American History X was Kaye's first directorial role in a feature film.

20
01:19:20,000 --> 01:19:26,000
Budgeted at $20 million, filming took place in 1997.

21
01:23:18,000 --> 01:23:24,000
Before the film's release, Kaye and New Line Cinema were in disagreements about the
final cut of the film, which Norton had played a pivotal role in editing.

22
01:27:16,000 --> 01:27:22,000
The final version was 40 minutes longer than Kaye's 95-minute cut, which resulted in him publicly disowning the
film through dozens of trade paper advertisements, thus negatively affecting his directing career.

23
01:31:14,000 --> 01:31:20,000
Distributed by New Line Cinema, the film was released in the United States on October 30, 1998 in limited theaters and
received a wide theatrical release on November 20, 1998, grossing $23.9 million against a $20 million budget.

24
01:35:12,000 --> 01:35:18,000
American History X was critically praised, with Norton and Furlong's
performances and the film's message drawing acclaim.

25
01:39:10,000 --> 01:39:16,000
Norton received an Academy Award nomination for Best Actor.

26
01:43:08,000 --> 01:43:14,000
The film has also been used for educational purposes in
the United States and in other countries.

27
01:47:06,000 --> 01:47:12,000
A follow-up, African History Y, with Kaye returning as director and
starring Djimon Hounsou, was in active development as of 2020.

28
01:51:04,000 --> 01:51:10,000
The film explores themes of prison, neo-nazism,
rape, skinhead, sibling relationship.

29
01:55:02,000 --> 01:55:08,000
The tone is dark and emotional.
